--- D-203 | 2026-11-01 | delivery-orchestrator
    DECISION (no separate rationale or reopen-if cell; 4 cells): "**Two automation findings from QA-H are repaired before the freeze, not accepted** (D-192: a Medium finding cannot be accepted as an observation).<br>• **O-1, throughput (Medium; REQ-S12-020, REQ-S20-029):** each worker process takes one job per queue every polling interval (`batchSize: 1`, `pollingIntervalSeconds: 2`, plus pg-boss 11's loop delay). A burst of N events therefore needs at least 2·N s; 1,000 import-accepted actuals would take at least 33 min. Repair: process a bounded batch per fetch with bounded concurrency, keep exactly-once (ADR-0043 three layers) and the per-rule locks, and measure before and after.<br>• **O-2, a rule acts on events that occurred before it was enabled (behaviour defined here; ADR-0043 is silent):** an automation rule applies only to events that occurred at or after the moment its effective version became enabled. An event that is late because of a backlog, a worker outage or a retry is evaluated against the rules that were enabled when it occurred, so enabling a rule never acts on history. This matches the versioning principle that a record keeps the configuration version in force when it was made (REQ-PB-093). The architect records it in ADR-0043.<br>• Owner: T-DG6-BE-X9. Then a full verification on the merged tree, then the freeze."
--- D-204 | 2026-11-01 | delivery-orchestrator
    DECISION (no separate rationale or reopen-if cell; 4 cells): "**The version-switch case of D-203 is decided: an event is evaluated against the rule version in force when it occurred.** BE-X9 (handback §6 item 2) implemented the narrower reading: an event that occurred under version N and was evaluated after version N+1 was published was acted on by neither version, so a backlog or outage at a publish would silently drop automations. Decision: evaluate a late event against the version that was effective and enabled at its occurrence time, found from the `automation_rule_enablement` ledger (0122). An event that occurred while the rule was disabled is never acted on (D-203 unchanged). Owner: T-DG6-BE-X10. The architect records D-203 and D-204 in ADR-0043 (T-DG6-ARCH-09)."
--- D-205 | 2026-11-01 | delivery-orchestrator
    DECISION (no separate rationale or reopen-if cell; 4 cells): "**BE-X10's kill-switch choice is confirmed:** a rule must also be enabled when the event is evaluated. Disabling a rule stops its not-yet-evaluated events immediately, including ones that occurred while it was enabled, so an administrator can stop a misbehaving rule during a backlog. D-204 is read accordingly: version in force and enabled at occurrence, **and** still enabled at evaluation. **Residual race accepted as documented, not hidden** (BE-X10 §8): a publish or enable transaction that starts before an event's transaction but commits after the event is evaluated can see the event evaluated against the earlier state. The ledger and outbox times are both transaction-start times. This is bounded by the evaluation lag and recorded in ADR-0043 (T-DG6-ARCH-10). It is not a Medium finding: no action is taken against a rule the administrator had not enabled at occurrence."
