--- D-209 | 2026-11-04 | delivery-orchestrator (design by solution-architect T-DG6-ARCH-14)
    DECISION (no separate rationale or reopen-if cell; 4 cells): "**A SecretRef resolves only for the setting IT provisioned it for (F-DG6-181).**<br>• IT declares each secret in a read-only JSON manifest `SECRET_BINDINGS_FILE` (outside the database, no values): one consumer kind (`connector`, `webhook_signing`, `smtp`, `storage`, `assistant`), one scope (an immutable organization code; `null` = installation for `storage` and `assistant`) and the 1–16 hosts it may be sent to (not for `webhook_signing`). Each `(provider, name)` appears once.<br>• Fail closed: no manifest, nothing bound; an invalid manifest stops the API and the worker at start.<br>• Write time, after authorization, shape and namespace: `400 validation` with field error `validation.secret_ref_not_bound` (identical for every cause) or `validation.secret_ref_host_not_bound`; audit `secret_ref.refused` in a separate transaction.<br>• Resolve time: `SecretProviderRegistry.resolve(ref, use)` is the only resolve; unbound returns undefined without asking the provider.<br>• `resolvable` is computed through the binding, so it is no existence oracle. The binding's hosts are the credential egress rule; redirects stay refused. No global endpoint allow-list is decided here (credential-less SSRF stays with the network egress policy; noted for a later round).<br>• No migration, no contract shape change. ADR-0052 §5 amendment of 2026-11-04. Implementer: T-DG6-BE-Z2."
